data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":0,"id":"writer-4_write","function":{"name":"write_artifact","arguments":""}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":0,"function":{"arguments":"{\"kind\":\"product_requirements\",\"title\":\"Product Requirements: Animated Weather App\",\"content\":\"## In short\\n- The first version is a **browser weather app whose whole screen animates to match the weather**, with location, saved cities, °C/°F, forecasts and side-by-side comparison. It is built the chosen way (Approach B): rich, realistic scenes.\\n- **60 must requirements** and 3 nice ones. Each must has a check in the acceptance criteria.\\n- Decisions to check: forecast covers **the next 24 hours and 7 days**; compare handles **up to 4 cities**; **saved cities are lost if browser data is cleared** and **older phones get a toned-down look** (both accepted by you); when offline we **show the last weather seen, clearly marked as old**.\\n\\n## Purpose\\nPeople who check the weather often should be able to glance at the screen and feel the conditions: rain falling, snow drifting, heat shimmering. Under the scenes sits the usual toolkit: where you are now, the places you care about, your preferred units and a quick comparison. Everything stays in the browser, with no sign-in.\\n\\n## Users\\n- **Regular weather checkers on a phone:** want the weather for where they are the moment they open the app, with smooth motion that does not drain the battery.\\n- **Desktop users:** want the same on a wide screen, and a clear way to compare several cities.\\n- **People who need less motion or have older devices:** want a calm or lighter version that still tells them the weather.\\n- **People using a keyboard or screen reader:** want every number and action reachable without the visuals.\\n\\n## Scope\\nCurrent location; city search; save and remove cities; °C/°F; current conditions; hourly and multi-day forecast; comparing cities; animated scenes for clear/hot, cloudy, rain, storm, snow, wind, fog, each by day and night; the loading, offline, permission-denied and no-results states; accessibility; home-screen install. Phone and desktop in current Chrome, Safari, Firefox and Edge.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- **Radar maps:** heavy to build and need another data source (brief).\\n- **Weather alerts:** need reliable official data (brief).\\n- **Accounts and sync across devices:** not needed; cities stay in one browser (you accepted this).\\n- **Native phone apps:** the brief asks only for a browser (constraints).\\n- **Our own server:** nothing about you is stored by us (chosen approach).\\n\\n## Functional requirements\\n\\n**Location and cities**\\n- **FR-1** On first open, the app asks for device location and, if allowed, shows weather for it. Must. Brief success criterion 1.\\n- **FR-2** The person can search for a city by typing, and sees matching places with name, region and country to tell same-named places apart. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-3** The person can open any search result to see its weather, and save it. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-4** The person can remove a saved city. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-5** Saved cities, the unit choice and the simpler-effects choice are still there when the app is reopened in the same browser. Must. Brief; Constraints, saved data.\\n- **FR-6** A saved cities list lets the person switch to a city with one tap or click, showing each city's current temperature and condition. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-7** The person can also use the current location as a place alongside saved cities and re-request it at any time. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-8** The same city cannot be saved twice. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-9** The local time at the chosen city is shown. Nice. Product judgement.\\n\\n**Units**\\n- **FR-10** One °C/°F toggle changes every temperature in the app (current, feels-like, hourly, daily, compare, saved list). Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-11** Wind speed and rain amounts follow the same toggle: km/h and mm with °C, mph and inches with °F. Must. Product judgement.\\n\\n**Weather content**\\n- **FR-12** Current conditions show temperature, feels-like, a plain-words description, wind speed and direction, humidity, and today's high and low. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-13** An hourly forecast shows the next 24 hours: time, temperature, condition and chance of rain. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-14** A daily forecast shows 7 days: day, condition, high, low and chance of rain. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-15** The app shows when the weather was last updated, and refreshes by itself when the person returns after 15 minutes or more away. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-16** A refresh button reloads the weather on demand. Must. Product judgement.\\n\\n**Comparing**\\n- **FR-17** The person can pick 2 to 4 saved cities and see them side by side, each with current conditions, feels-like, wind, humidity, and today's high and low. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-18** In comparison, each city also shows its condition icon and a short next-hours outlook so differences read at a glance. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-19** On a narrow phone screen the comparison stays readable, by scrolling sideways or stacking, without squeezing text below a legible size. Must. Brief, phone and desktop.\\n- **FR-20** Each compared city's card is tinted to its own weather. Nice. Product judgement.\\n\\n**Animated scenes**\\n- **FR-21** The full-screen background shows a scene matching the current city's conditions: clear/hot, cloudy, rain, storm, snow, wind or fog. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-22** Every scene has a day and a night version, chosen from sunrise and sunset **at that city**, not the viewer's clock. Must. Brief.\\n- **FR-23** At full quality, scenes use rich realistic motion: many individual raindrops with splashes, drifting snowflakes with depth, wind-blown streaks and bending objects, moving cloud layers, lightning flashes in storms, heat shimmer in hot clear weather, and low rolling mist in fog. Must. Chosen approach B.\\n- **FR-24** Different conditions look clearly different from one another, including at reduced quality. Must. Brief success criterion 2.\\n- **FR-25** Heavier or lighter weather changes the scene's intensity (drizzle versus downpour, flurries versus heavy snow). Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-26** When the person changes city, the scene changes to the new weather with a short smooth transition. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-27** Weather text and numbers sit on soft panels over the scene so they stay readable. Must. Chosen approach B.\\n\\n**Keeping it smooth**\\n- **FR-28** The app measures how smoothly it runs and automatically lowers the number of particles and effects if it struggles; it may raise them again if the device recovers. Must. Chosen approach B.\\n- **FR-29** Animation stops when the tab is hidden or the screen is locked, and resumes when the person returns. Must. Chosen approach B.\\n- **FR-30** If the device asks for reduced motion, scenes are calm or still, with the weather shown by colour and gentle visuals, and no falling, flashing or shimmering motion. Must. Brief; chosen approach B.\\n- **FR-31** A visible \\\"simpler effects\\\" switch lets the person choose lighter effects on any device, and the choice is remembered. Must. Chosen approach B.\\n- **FR-32** Scenes load after the weather figures appear, so numbers are never held up by the graphics. Must. Chosen approach B.\\n\\n**States**\\n- **FR-33** While weather loads, the app shows a loading state (placeholders where the numbers will go) rather than a blank or stale screen. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-34** When the device is offline, the app shows the last weather it saved for that city, clearly labelled with its age, and says fresh data needs internet. If there is none, it says so. Must. Constraints, assumptions.\\n- **FR-35** If the person declines or blocks location, the app says so in plain words and offers city search, and shows saved cities if any. Must. Constraints, privacy.\\n- **FR-36** A search with no matches says \\\"no results\\\" and suggests checking the spelling or trying a nearby larger city. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-37** If the weather service fails or is slow, the app shows a plain error with a retry button, keeping any older data visible and labelled. Must. Constraints, free-service risk.\\n- **FR-38** On a first visit with location declined and no saved cities, the home screen shows a friendly prompt to search, with a calm default background. Must. Product judgement.\\n- **FR-39** The app can be added to a phone or computer home screen and opens like an app. Must. Constraints, solution form.\\n\\n## Non-functional requirements\\n- **NFR-1** Text and key numbers meet WCAG 2.2 AA contrast (4.5:1 for normal text) over every scene, day and night. Must. Constraints.\\n- **NFR-2** Every action works with a keyboard alone, with a visible focus marker and a sensible tab order. Must. Constraints.\\n- **NFR-3** A screen reader can read the current conditions, forecasts and comparison, and the scene's meaning is available as text (for example \\\"Heavy rain, night\\\"). Must. Constraints.\\n- **NFR-4** Lightning and flashing effects never flash more than 3 times in any second, and are off under reduced motion. Must. Product judgement (safety).\\n- **NFR-5** At full quality, scenes run at about 30 frames a second or better on a phone from the last four to five years; below that, quality scaling steps down. Must. Constraints, mid-range phone.\\n- **NFR-6** On a typical mobile connection, current figures appear within 3 seconds of opening; scenes may follow after. Must. Chosen approach B risk (download size).\\n- **NFR-7** Hidden-tab animation uses effectively no processing. Must. Chosen approach B.\\n- **NFR-8** The layout works from 320 px wide phones to wide desktop screens, with no sideways scrolling except in comparison. Must. Brief.\\n- **NFR-9** The app works in current Chrome, Safari, Firefox and Edge on desktop and phone. Must. Constraints.\\n- **NFR-10** The app collects nothing about the person. Device location and typed city names go only to the weather and city-lookup services, and nothing else is stored beyond the browser. Must. Constraints, privacy.\\n- **NFR-11** The app needs no account or key from the person and has no running cost at personal scale. Must. Constraints.\\n- **NFR-12** Interface wording is plain and non-technical, including error messages. Must. Person's preference.\\n- **NFR-13** Weather requests are reused for a few minutes (for example when switching between cities) to stay within free-service limits. Nice. Constraints, usage limits.\\n- **NFR-14** An in-app note explains that saved cities live only in this browser and disappear if browser data is cleared. Must. You accepted this trade-off; it should not be a surprise.\\n- **NFR-15** On the weakest devices the scenes may be much simpler, but numbers, forecasts and comparison work the same. Must. You accepted this trade-off.\\n\\n## Interface requirements\\n- **IR-1 Home screen:** the animated scene, city name, current conditions, hourly strip, 7-day list, unit toggle, search entry, refresh and last-updated time. Must. Brief.\\n- **IR-2 Search:** a text box with live results below, and the no-results and error states. Must. Brief.\\n- **IR-3 Saved cities:** a list showing each city's temperature and condition, with remove and \\\"compare\\\" selection. Must. Brief.\\n- **IR-4 Compare:** side-by-side view of 2 to 4 cities. Must. Brief.\\n- **IR-5 Settings:** °C/°F, simpler effects, a reduce-motion explanation, and the saved-in-browser note. Must. Brief; chosen approach B.\\n- **IR-6 Weather data:** Open-Meteo forecast service, with no key, for current, hourly, daily, sunrise/sunset and weather codes. If it falls short, another free service replaces it. Must. Constraints.\\n- **IR-7 City lookup:** Open-Meteo's city search, returning name, region, country and coordinates. Must. Constraints.\\n- **IR-8 Device location:** the browser's location feature, asked only on first open or when the person taps \\\"use my location\\\". Must. Constraints.\\n- **IR-9 Browser storage:** saved cities, unit, simpler-effects choice and last weather seen, kept in the browser on that device. Must. Constraints.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- **AC-1** (FR-1, IR-8) Open the app fresh and allow location: weather for that place appears with a matching scene. The prompt is not shown again on later opens unless re-requested.\\n- **AC-2** (FR-2, IR-7) Type \\\"Springfield\\\": several results appear, each with region and country.\\n- **AC-3** (FR-3) Choose a result: its weather opens, and a save action adds it to the saved list.\\n- **AC-4** (FR-4) Remove a saved city: it disappears at once and stays gone after reload.\\n- **AC-5** (FR-5, IR-9) Save two cities, set °F and simpler effects, close and reopen the browser: all are still set.\\n- **AC-6** (FR-6) The saved list shows each city's temperature and condition; tapping a city switches the home screen to it.\\n- **AC-7** (FR-7) With location allowed, \\\"My location\\\" appears alongside saved cities; tapping \\\"use my location\\\" re-checks it.\\n- **AC-8** (FR-8) Trying to save an already saved city does not create a second entry.\\n- **AC-9** (FR-10) Toggle to °F: every temperature on every screen changes, and 0 °C reads 32 °F.\\n- **AC-10** (FR-11) Toggle units: wind switches between km/h and mph and rain between mm and inches.\\n- **AC-11** (FR-12) Current conditions show every listed item for a real city and match Open-Meteo's figures for it.\\n- **AC-12** (FR-13) The hourly strip shows 24 entries starting from the current hour, each with the four listed items.\\n- **AC-13** (FR-14) The daily list shows 7 days with the five listed items.\\n- **AC-14** (FR-15) The last-updated time shows. Leave the tab for 15+ minutes and return: weather refreshes without a tap.\\n- **AC-15** (FR-16) Tapping refresh reloads data and updates the time.\\n- **AC-16** (FR-17, FR-18, IR-4) Select 2, 3 and 4 saved cities: all appear side by side with the listed data, condition icons and outlook; a 5th cannot be selected.\\n- **AC-17** (FR-19, NFR-8) At 360 px wide the comparison is readable with no cut-off text.\\n- **AC-18** (FR-21, FR-24) Force each of the seven conditions with test data: each shows its own scene, and a reviewer shown them unlabelled can tell all seven apart, with the check repeated under simpler effects.\\n- **AC-19** (FR-22) A city in daylight and one at night (for example Sydney and London at the same moment) show day and night scenes respectively, whatever the viewer's clock says.\\n- **AC-20** (FR-23) At full quality on a desktop, rain shows many drops and splashes, snow shows depth, storms show lightning, clear hot weather shows shimmer, wind moves clouds and objects, and fog shows mist.\\n- **AC-21** (FR-25) Light and heavy rain test data look visibly different in density.\\n- **AC-22** (FR-26) Switching between a sunny and a rainy city transitions smoothly with no blank flash.\\n- **AC-23** (FR-27, NFR-1) Contrast of all text over each scene, day and night, measures at least 4.5:1.\\n- **AC-24** (FR-28, NFR-5) On a throttled test device, the app drops its particle count within a few seconds and the frame rate recovers; on a mid-range phone it holds about 30 frames a second.\\n- **AC-25** (FR-29, NFR-7) Switch tabs: animation work stops (shown by the browser's performance tool), and it resumes on return.\\n- **AC-26** (FR-30, NFR-4) With the device's reduce-motion setting on, no scene has falling, flashing or shimmering motion, and the weather is still identifiable. In storm at full quality, lightning never flashes more than 3 times in a second.\\n- **AC-27** (FR-31) Turn on simpler effects: particle numbers visibly drop on a strong device, and the setting persists after reload.\\n- **AC-28** (FR-32, NFR-6) On a throttled mobile connection, figures show within 3 seconds, before the scene finishes loading.\\n- **AC-29** (FR-33) On a slow connection a loading state with placeholders shows, never a blank screen.\\n- **AC-30** (FR-34) Go offline after viewing a city: reopen and see its last weather labelled with its age; an unseen city shows an offline message.\\n- **AC-31** (FR-35, IR-8) Block location: a plain message appears, search is offered, and saved cities still show.\\n- **AC-32** (FR-36) Search \\\"qzxwv\\\": a no-results message with suggestions appears.\\n- **AC-33** (FR-37) With the weather service blocked, a plain error and retry appear, and retry works once the block is lifted.\\n- **AC-34** (FR-38) First visit, location declined, no saved cities: search prompt on a calm background.\\n- **AC-35** (FR-39) The browser offers to install it, and the installed app opens full screen.\\n- **AC-36** (NFR-2, NFR-3) Using only the keyboard, search, save, compare, toggle units and change settings; a screen reader announces conditions, forecast, comparison and scene text.\\n- **AC-37** (NFR-9) Checks AC-1 to AC-9 pass in current Chrome, Safari, Firefox and Edge, on desktop and phone.\\n- **AC-38** (NFR-10, NFR-11, IR-6) Watching network traffic shows requests only to the weather and city-lookup services and the host; no sign-in is requested.\\n- **AC-39** (NFR-12) A reviewer who is not technical reads every message and finds none confusing.\\n- **AC-40** (NFR-14, IR-5) The settings screen shows the note about browser-only saving, plus every listed setting.\\n- **AC-41** (NFR-15) On a deliberately weak test device, the numbers, forecasts and comparison all work with a simpler scene.\\n- **AC-42** (IR-1, IR-2, IR-3) The home, search and saved-cities screens exist with their listed contents.\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n- The forecast covers 24 hours and 7 days, as the brief gave no lengths.\\n- Compare allows up to 4 cities, so columns stay readable on a phone.\\n- Units: °C goes with km/h and mm, °F with mph and inches, from one toggle.\\n- Default unit on first open follows the device's region, then is remembered.\\n- A refresh after 15 minutes away is enough to keep the weather current.\\n- The last weather seen is kept for offline use, and shown with its age.\\n- A \\\"mid-range phone\\\" is one from the last four to five years (constraints).\\n- \\\"Weakest devices\\\" means those where scaling reaches its lowest setting; they may get a still or simple scene.\\n- Heat scene applies to clear weather above about 30 °C; this threshold can be changed.\\n- Weather types such as hail and drizzle map to the nearest of the seven scenes, with intensity adjusted.\\n- Saved cities are lost if browser data is cleared, and older phones get a toned-down look (both accepted by you).\\n\\n## Sources\\nProblem Brief: Animated Weather App; Approaches: Animated Weather App (Approach B chosen); Constraints: Animated Weather App.\\n\"}"}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

